  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Does the Moon make its own light?",
      "a": "No. The Moon is a rocky ball with no fire or glow of its own. We see it because sunlight bounces off it and some of that light reaches our eyes.",
      "asks": [
        "Is moonlight real light?",
        "Where does moonlight come from?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How much light does the Moon reflect?",
      "a": "The Moon reflects only about 12 percent of the sunlight that hits it, about as much as worn tarmac. It looks bright because it is lit by the full Sun against a black sky.",
      "asks": [
        "Is the Moon bright or dark?",
        "What colour is the Moon really?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is earthshine?",
      "a": "Earthshine is sunlight that bounces off Earth onto the Moon and back again. It faintly lights the dark part of a crescent Moon, an effect sometimes called the old Moon in the new Moon's arms.",
      "asks": [
        "Why can I see the dark part of the Moon?",
        "Why does the dark side of a crescent Moon glow?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How long does moonlight take to reach us?",
      "a": "Light travels about 300 thousand kilometres every second, and the Moon is about 384 thousand kilometres away. So moonlight takes about 1.3 seconds to reach your eyes.",
      "asks": [
        "How far away is the Moon in light seconds?",
        "Is moonlight old light?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the Moon change shape if it is always lit?",
      "a": "Half of the Moon is always lit by the Sun. As the Moon goes round Earth each month, we see different amounts of that lit half, which gives the phases from crescent to full.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the Moon have phases?",
        "Why is the Moon sometimes a crescent?"
      ]
    }
